--- log ---
2026-05-11T20:30Z claude open — design proposal from author during audit lint session
2026-05-12T08:00Z MinhHaDuong decide — yes, pursue; principle is "URL to a ticket" with shortcuts; focus on spec first, resolver rewrite is a followup

--- body ---
## Proposal

Reinterpret Blocked-by refs as URLs with progressive locality. The
shortest form is the most local, longer forms reach further:

| Form | Meaning | Resolution |
|------|---------|------------|
| 0042 | Same directory | Current behavior |
| module/0042 | Sibling directory under tickets/ | Walk up to tickets/, check sibling |
| path/tickets/0042 | Cross-project, same machine | Absolute or relative path |
| host/owner/repo#N | Cross-project, cross-machine | Current forge ref behavior |

## Motivation

Supports multi-module monorepos where several subdirectories each have
their own tickets/ store under a shared root. Currently these cannot
cross-reference each other without using forge refs (which require a
remote and are blocking-by-default offline).

## Design questions

1. What is the resolution algorithm? Walk up from current ticket dir
   to find a common tickets/ ancestor?
2. Does `module/0042` mean "the tickets/ directory named module" or
   "the directory module which contains a tickets/ subdirectory"?
3. How does this interact with erg check cycle detection? Cross-module
   cycles need a different algorithm than single-directory DFS.
4. Is this a v1.x addition (additive, unknown forms already rejected)
   or a v2 change?
5. Does this need a resolver that walks the filesystem, violating the
   current "erg never does I/O beyond the ticket store" contract?

## Decision needed

Author decides whether this is worth pursuing and which form the
shortcut hierarchy takes. The current 4-digit-only local ref is
simple and well-tested; this would be a significant complexity increase.
